Z||000014||terryk||12-20-2000||03:44 PM||||That engine in the picture in just an air sucking 3800. If there is nitrous, they forgot to mention that. The GN's with those HUGH numbers are running 25+ PSI of boost. That engine isn't doing that. Apples and oranges.<P>As for the blondes and trash, I'm with Mshill. The only one's that can afford to build a replica are the one's that have the money.......Otherwise, we drive Fiero's.....||||reg||1
Z||000015||88formula||12-20-2000||06:51 PM||||Here is how a small engine builds lots of horsepower.<P>1, Turbocharger<P>2, Supercharger<P>3, Nitrous<P>4, High Rpm’s<P>That engine probably makes 340 horsepower by high rpm's just like Honda's four cylinder in the S2000.<P>I just took a look at the engine. That is an 87 or older 3800 engine (3.8-liter) with a restrictive mass air flow sensor and port fuel injection. I had an 89 Bonneville with the 165 horsepower 3800 engine. The 87 engine was rated @ 150 horsepower from the factory. I seriously doubt that that engine makes 340 horsepower with that setup. <P>It must be a Convenient typo.||||reg||1
Z||000016||rogergarrison||12-20-2000||06:59 PM||||i guess i was wrong on Skyline, i thought they were 4 cyl, but 450 is still pretty damn good for 6- even though I hate rice burners. Mine was checked on engine dyno after i got turbo installed, was just short of 275. Im VERY VERY HAPPY with my performance, glad i didnt opt for a V-8 conv. Ill freeway race just about anything that pulls beside me, Im not intimidated. I just got done painting a 88 Vette with a almost new 350 auto and took it for a test spin (belongs to a dealer) my lowly Ferrari/Fiero can eat it alive.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1234884&a=9116379&p=34556367"> ||||reg||1
